DO  - 10.1007/s10304-023-00532-3
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85173664820&doi=10.1007%2fs10304-023-00532-3&partnerID=40&md5=3ce2e1d0646b439c2882fe9f0c131a40
AB  - Background: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a frequent disease in women of reproductive age and a leading cause of subfertility. The PCOS has a complex pathophysiology and diverse clinical symptoms, including chronic low-grade inflammation. In this context, understanding the immune alterations in PCOS opens the possibility to personalize the treatment to the individual patient’s symptomatology as well as to develop new treatment approaches. Objective: The aim of this minireview is to summarize and contextualize solid observations on the immune deregulation in PCOS patients and to provide considerations on their impact on current treatment concepts. Material and methods: In this literature review relevant publications available in PubMed were extracted. Fundamental research studies, systematic reviews, and expert recommendations were discussed. Results: Significant immune alterations were associated with PCOS at the systemic, ovarian and endometrial levels. These alterations explain elevated markers of immune activation, which may contribute to the worsening of reproductive symptoms and comorbidities associated with PCOS. Conclusion: Insights into the impact of the immune system on the pathophysiology of PCOS enable a targeted selection of treatment and form a foundation for the long-term monitoring of this population of affected women. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Medizin Verlag GmbH, ein Teil von Springer Nature.
