TI  - In vitro evaluation of the antidiabetic efficacy of mushrooms
PY  - 2023
T2  - Therapeutic Mushrooms for Diabetes Mellitus: Current Evidences and Future Scope
SP  - 189
EP  - 216
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85162174916&partnerID=40&md5=6ffc34552bbc7e581a54fe65b64dc613
AB  - Medicinal mushrooms have been valued as a traditional source of natural bioactive compounds over several decades and have been targeted as potential hypoglycemic and antidiabetic agents. A large number of in vitro studies have reported significant antidiabetic efficacy of a wide array of mushroom extracts and isolated mycoconstituents that impart their antidiabetic effects by modulating the activity of enzymes like α-glycosidase and aldose reductase: displaying insulin-like or insulin potentiating action: and DPPH-free radical scavenging activity that potentially neutralizes the oxidized "advanced glycated end products" (AGEs). The significant in vitro hypoglycemic effect and the corresponding in vivo antidiabetic activity exhibited by various mushrooms investigated emphasizes the fact that these macrofungi could potentially serve as a promising source of medicine and have the potential to serve as functional food and in addition the extract and its fractions may be explored for the development of natural product-based pharmaceuticals. © 2023 Apple Academic Press, Inc. All rights reserved.
