TI  - DNA methylation analysis reveals the effect of arsenic on gestational diabetes mellitus
PY  - 2023
T2  - Genomics
VL  - 115
IS  - 5
C7  - 110674
DO  - 10.1016/j.ygeno.2023.110674
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85165600341&doi=10.1016%2fj.ygeno.2023.110674&partnerID=40&md5=8804b2f625b27947cb87c7e3c2a7bbeb
AB  - Background: Arsenic (As) exposure is one of the risk factors for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). This study aimed to explore the effect of As-exposure on DNA methylation in GDM and to establish a risk assessment model of GDM in As exposed pregnant women. Method: We collected elbow vein blood of pregnant women before delivery to measure As concentration and DNA methylation data. Then compared the DNA methylation data and established a nomogram. Result: We identified a total of 10 key differentially methylated CpGs (DMCs) and found 6 corresponding genes. Functions were enriched in Hippo signaling pathway, cell tight junction, prophetic acid metabolism, ketone body metabolic process, and antigen processing and presentation. A nomogram was established that can predict GDM risks (c-index = 0.595, s:p = 0.973). Conclusion: We found 6 genes associated with GDM with high As exposure. The prediction of the nomograms has been proven to be effective. © 2023 The Authors
