UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85168081265&doi=10.1007%2fs11428-023-01079-4&partnerID=40&md5=71dd03b7f39587916fe7533b9b634731
AB  - Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR; also referred to fetal growth retardation) occurs in about 5–8% of all pregnancies and is often caused by insufficient intrauterine supply. The diagnosis is not made based on birth weight, but on serial ultrasound measurements documenting that intrauterine growth is shifting downward and crossing percentiles for gestational age. Since IUGR increases the risk for the development of certain metabolic and nonmetabolic diseases, the diagnosis of IUGR should not only be communicated between obstetricians and neonatologists, but pediatric, general medicine and internal medicine colleagues treating the patient should also be informed. Whether the elevated risk develops into manifest disease depends on numerous internal and external factors during life. During certain windows of development like the early postnatal period, individual adaption to environmental factors (e.g., duration of breastfeeding, nutrient composition of early diet) can help to prevent secondary disease throughout the lifespan. The present review highlights etiology, diagnostic work-up, and management of IUGR. In addition, we present epidemiological and experimental research which illustrates the association between IUGR and later metabolic disease. Furthermore, we discuss preconceptional and prenatal strategies to prevent IUGR and outline approaches for postnatal strategies to prevent metabolic disease after IUGR. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Medizin Verlag GmbH, ein Teil von Springer Nature.
