AB  - In recent years, there is a hypothesis that halophytes, plants that thrive in extremely salty environments, may secrete bioactive compounds that may help reduce the negative effects of stress. These phytochemicals might be superior starting materials for creating new medicines. The goal of this study was to use three distinct solvents to describe the physiologically active compounds found in Heliotropium curassavicum L. which exhibited antimicrobial and anti-diabetic properties. Common phytochemical screening assays, an in vitro antidiabetic assay (using Alpha-glucosidase and Alpha-amylase), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy, gas chromatography-mass spectrometry, and a toxicity study utilizing brine shrimp were all used to analyze bioactive compounds. The plant extracts' antimicrobial efficacy was tested using the well diffusion method. Ethyl acetate extract of H.curassavicum contained the highest potency of Alkaloids, saponins, phenolics, flavonoids, steroids, cardiac glycosides, tannins, and terpenoids are examples of bioactive phytocompounds. The results of the antimicrobial assays revealed that at a concentration of 50 mg/ml, all three plant extracts demonstrated significant susceptibility to Bacillus cereus, Escherichia coli, and Staphylococcus aureus among the pathogens tested. Invitro antidiabetic study revealed that ethyl acetate extract exhibits 60% of inhibition with a IC 50 value of 0.36 ± 06, The IC 50 value of the standard was 0.2319 mg/mL. The current study provides insight into the potential of halophytes as a natural source of secondary metabolites and bioactive chemicals with antimicrobial and anti-diabetic properties. © 2023
