TI  - Low-dose spironolactone attenuates metabolic defects in the skeletal muscle of letrozole-induced hyperandrogenic female rats
PY  - 2023
T2  - Nutrire
VL  - 48
IS  - 1
C7  - 20
DO  - 10.1186/s41110-023-00205-y
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85152660409&doi=10.1186%2fs41110-023-00205-y&partnerID=40&md5=50b10222f31a1a03282212d0cd42f8c5
AB  - Purpose: Hyperandrogenism is characterized by high levels of testosterone, which has been reported to cause insulin resistance and other metabolic adversities. Spironolactone (SPL) is a mineralocorticoid receptor blocker with anti-inflammatory effects. However, it is unknown whether SPL would restore skeletal muscle defect against letrozole-induced hyperandrogenism. Therefore, the present study investigated the hypothesis that low-dose SPL would attenuate skeletal muscle defect in hyperandrogenic female rats. Methods: Eight-week-old female Wistar rats were divided into three groups, namely, control, letrozole (LET)-treated, and LET + SPL-treated groups. The control received vehicle, LET-treated group received 1 mg/kg of LET, and LET + SPL-treated group received a combination of LET and SPL (0.25 mg/kg). The administrations were done by oral gavage for 21 days uninterruptedly. Biochemical analyses were determined with appropriate assay methods. Results: The results of this experiment showed that letrozole increased plasma testosterone and leptin and caused insulin resistance and increased triglyceride, total cholesterol, free fatty acids, pro-inflammatory markers (TNF-α and NF-κB), lipid peroxidation markers (MDA), and apoptosis in the skeletal muscle. Nevertheless, administration of low-dose SPL attenuated these alterations in skeletal muscles. Conclusion: The present study demonstrates that letrozole-induced hyperandrogenism causes metabolic-driven apoptosis in the skeletal muscles. Hence, low-dose SPL mitigates metabolic defect and apoptosis in the skeletal muscles of hyperandrogenic female rats. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
