TI  - CTRP1: A novel player in cardiovascular and metabolic diseases
PY  - 2023
T2  - Cytokine
VL  - 164
C7  - 156162
DO  - 10.1016/j.cyto.2023.156162
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85148374425&doi=10.1016%2fj.cyto.2023.156162&partnerID=40&md5=33c10c7c0a5ed3a70c9b1e831a31806a
AB  - Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are a series of diseases induced by inflammation and lipid metabolism disorders, among others. Metabolic diseases can cause inflammation and abnormal lipid metabolism. C1q/TNF-related proteins 1 (CTRP1) is a paralog of adiponectin that belongs to the CTRP subfamily. CTRP1 is expressed and secreted in adipocytes, macrophages, cardiomyocytes, and other cells. It promotes lipid and glucose metabolism but has bidirectional effects on the regulation of inflammation. Inflammation can also inversely stimulate CTRP1 production. A vicious circle may exist between the two. This article introduces CTRP1 from the structure, expression, and different roles of CTRP1 in CVDs and metabolic diseases, to summarize the role of CTRP1 pleiotropy. Moreover, the proteins which may interact with CTRP1 are predicted through GeneCards and STRING, speculating their effects, to provide new ideas for the study of CTRP1. © 2023
