AB  - Probiotic microorganisms are defined as “living microorganisms, which upon ingestion in certain numbers, exert health benefits beyond inherent basic nutrition,” and most of them belong to lactic acid bacteria (LAB), for instance, Bifidobacterium sp., Lactobacillus sp., and Enterococcus sp. LAB are a varied category of Gram-positive, non-spore-forming, catalase-negative bacteria that can be found in various environments, including plants and animals. Researchers approve that probiotic must be able to survive transport over the harsh gastrointestinal (GI) tract to exert beneficial impacts on the colon, even though there is considerable information on useful immunological impacts also from dead cells. LAB are the most important probiotic microorganisms because they are autochthonous in the human gastrointestinal tract of healthy people. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2023.
