TI  - Endocrine disruptors in the pathogenesis of metabolic syndrome
PY  - 2023
T2  - Metabolic Syndrome: From Mechanisms to Interventions
SP  - 235
EP  - 248
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-323-85732-1.00018-9
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85186980065&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-323-85732-1.00018-9&partnerID=40&md5=ba7cc739b10a681ce1ca332ccc9db5d7
AB  - The prevalence of Metabolic Syndrome is increasing worldwide. Apart from the known risk factors for the development of the Metabolic Syndrome, focus is now shifting to the role of exposure to chemicals found in the environment or used in industries labeled as endocrine disruptors to account for this increase. These agents can interfere with any aspect of hormone action and lead to disruption in various metabolic processes. Effects of endocrine disruptors depend on various factors including age at exposure, window of exposure, source or the number of substances involved. Prenatal exposure may lead to defective programming of endocrine signaling pathways, while postnatal exposure can affect food intake, glucose and lipid homeostasis, pancreatic beta cells, or insulin sensitivity. Most studies of endocrine disruptors have focused on animal studies and epidemiological data in humans. However, large prospective studies are needed to establish a cause-and-effect relationship in susceptible populations. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
