UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85180048348&doi=10.1201%2f9781003384823-20&partnerID=40&md5=d1af5113941af3814ee93a0bb9bd9c7b
AB  - A category of illnesses known as metabolic diseases disrupt the body’s metabolism, which is the process by which food is turned into energy that the body can use. Genetic mutations, dietary variables, or a mix of both can cause metabolic disorders. Therapy for metabolic illnesses often entails dietary and exercise changes, medication, and occasionally surgery. These disorders can help prevent problems and enhance quality of life with early detection and management. Many infections have been linked to the emergence of metabolic disorders. Although viruses have been associated with metabolic illnesses, it is crucial to remember that genetics, lifestyle choices, and environmental variables are equally important in the emergence of these conditions. Furthermore, not everyone who comes into contact with these bacteria will acquire metabolic disorders. To fully comprehend the connection between infections and metabolic illnesses, more study is required. It has been established that the billions of bacteria that inhabit the human digestive tract, known as the gut microbiota, are crucial in the emergence and treatment of metabolic illnesses. According to certain studies, dysbiosis - an imbalance in the gut microbiota - may play a role in the emergence of metabolic illnesses like diabetes, obesity, and metabolic syndrome. This chapter focuses on details about the common pathogens involved in metabolic disorders. © 2024 selection and editorial matter, Seth K. Amponsah, Emmanuel K. Ofori, Yashwant V. Pathak; individual chapters, the contributors.
