UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85152830013&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-323-89847-8.00020-1&partnerID=40&md5=7299589fe585e9471c1574b9fa90f8e7
AB  - Arsenic (As) is a ubiquitous element found in various environmental sources including water, air, soil, plants, and foodstuffs. The analysis of As is challenging, as it is readily metabolized and undergoes biotransformation which depends upon valence state and presence of methylated arsenicals. Physical, chemical, and biological variables such as pH, redox conditions, speciation, enzymatic activity, and genetic polymorphism significantly affect the analysis and assessment of As in vivo and in vitro. This chapter summarizes the occurrence of arsenic in various food products and crops along with the factors affecting biological and geological availability of As. There are few standard biomarkers to assess the presence of arsenicals in cellular system, however, the correlation of these biomarkers with the risk analysis is of great concern. Integrated modeling approach considering variation in exposure, duration, lifespan, and nutritional status may develop significant understanding of risk analysis of As exposure. The objective behind developing various risk assessment models and correlating them with biological and geographical factors is to define and clarify the extent of risk associated with As exposure. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
