EP  - 295
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-323-91294-5.00008-7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85160076181&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-323-91294-5.00008-7&partnerID=40&md5=96e24d6a9dcd2f2ea0a36a82eca0f4d9
AB  - Diabetes mellitus (DM) is a complicated condition that impacts patients' everyday lives and increases their chance of acquiring related disorders. There is a high need for novel antidiabetic medicines to treat DM. As chemical drugs have various side effects such as obesity, gastrointestinal problems, and cardiovascular diseases, now herbal medicines are the best choice among people. Traditional herbal medicine is a fantastic branch of study for discovering novel drugs. Currently, alternative methods to managing diabetes, such as diet management, physical activity, and antidiabetic plant-derived molecules/foods, are recommended since they are less expensive and have fewer or no adverse effects. Various plant-derived phytocompounds are used to treat DM by targeting metabolic pathways. To better understand their roles, it is necessary to analyze the structure-activity relationship (SAR). As a result, studies were undertaken to explore the association between its structures and their antioxidant and antidiabetic properties. Further investigation into this field may lead to the formation of nutritional products and semisynthetic analogs with significant antidiabetic properties and few side effects. The study of the SAR model could attain knowledge about the compound and its impact on DM. SAR model analysis emphasizes the detection of ligand binding pocket along with its chemical structure for treating DM. © 2023 Elsevier B.V.
