TI  - Role of Probiotics in the Prevention and Management of Obesity: What Have We Learned So Far?
PY  - 2023
T2  - Biomedical Research, Medicine, and Disease
SP  - 377
EP  - 393
DO  - 10.1201/9781003220404-27
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85161879684&doi=10.1201%2f9781003220404-27&partnerID=40&md5=8f87eccef7fee972998c6d0f4b8ccd63
AB  - Obesity has posed a global health challenge and is increasing at an alarming pace among children and adults both in developed and developing countries. There are multiple factors involved in causing obesity, such as unhealthy eating habits, excessive use of carbohydrates and sugar-loaded drinks, low consumption of fruits and vegetables, high consumption of saturated fat and red meat, sedentary lifestyle, work environment, urban living, growth of fast-food chains, endocrine disrupters, genetics, psychogenic factors, etc. Recent observations indicate that the gut microbiota seem to play an important role in regulating energy homeostasis in our body. Obesity is the mother of several non-communicable diseases such as diabetes mellitus, cardiovascular and neurodegenerative diseases, osteoarthritis, depression, and premature death, just to name a few. Diet- and environment-induced alterations in the gut microbiota are associated with the development of obesity. Probiotics consist of live bacteria that confer health benefits on the host. They may reduce symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome, some types of inflammatory bowel disease, and diarrhoea from antibiotics. Probiotics containing Lactobacillus and/or Bifidobacterium have been noted to improve lipid profile, decrease body weight, and metabolic dysfunction in animal models and obese humans. The focus of this book chapter is to summarize the potential mechanisms of high-fat diet-induced dysbiosis and the consequent occurrence of obesity. The role of probiotics in combating obesity and related metabolic disorders in experimental and clinical settings is also discussed. © 2023 selection and editorial matter, R.C. Sobti and Aastha Sobti; individual chapters, the contributors.
