TI  - Abdominal obesity increases metabolic risk factors in obese versus non-obese adults in Karbala
PY  - 2022
T2  - AIP Conference Proceedings
VL  - 2547
C7  - 020026
DO  - 10.1063/5.0113497
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85144185869&doi=10.1063%2f5.0113497&partnerID=40&md5=840c01e06cd71d0c2d242c603fb519e4
AB  - Obesity is a global phenomenon that causes a slew of health issues, including type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular disease. Methods: This research was carried out at Karbala's Al-Hussein general teaching hospital. The samples were collected between the months of August 2020 and March 2021. The Case-control study included 80 obese patients and 28 healthy people from the obesity clinic at the hospital in question. A total of 31 males and 49 females were infected with metabolic syndrome. The patients' ages ranged from 31 to 65 years old. The requirements for working were established. Those who met at least three of the Adult Treatment Panel III (ATP III) metabolic syndrome requirements were assigned to a category. Serum levels of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and insulin were measured. Results The findings indicate a substantial decrease in serum levels of atrial natriuretic peptide hormone in obese and non-obese patients as compared to controls in both sexes (P value 0.05). Mean levels of ANP showed a significant difference between patients with Mets morbidly obesity (28.714.50) mg/dl and safe subjects (58.722.51) mg/dl. There is a strong negative association between the atrial natriuretic peptide test and total serum glucose levels. Conclusions Diabetes is linked to lower natriuretic peptide levels in both non obese and obese people. These results suggest that low levels of ANP could be a symptom of obesity, with potential pathophysiological implications.  © 2022 Author(s).
