TI  - Diabetes and skin cancers: Risk factors, molecular mechanisms and impact on prognosis
PY  - 2022
T2  - World Journal of Clinical Cases
VL  - 10
IS  - 31
SP  - 11214
EP  - 11225
DO  - 10.12998/wjcc.v10.i31.11214
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85141778480&doi=10.12998%2fwjcc.v10.i31.11214&partnerID=40&md5=1710f777f4f4ba9d80885aa158b33919
AB  - Diabetes and skin cancers have emerged as threats to public health worldwide. However, their association has been less intensively studied. In this narrative review, we explore the common risk factors, molecular mechanisms, and prognosis of the association between cutaneous malignancies and diabetes. Hyperglycemia, oxidative stress, low-grade chronic inflammation, genetic, lifestyle, and environmental factors partially explain the crosstalk between skin cancers and this metabolic disorder. In addition, diabetes and its related complications may interfere with the appropriate management of cutaneous malignancies. Antidiabetic medication seems to exert an antineoplastic effect, however, future large, observation studies with a prospective design are needed to clarify its impact on the risk of malignancy in diabetes. Screening for diabetes in skin cancers, as well as close follow-up for the development of cutaneous malignancies in subjects suffering from diabetes, is warranted. © The Author(s) 2022. Published by Baishideng Publishing Group Inc. All rights reserved.
