TI  - Cardiovascular consequences of human exposure to phthalates
PY  - 2022
T2  - Phthalates: Environmental and Health Effects
SP  - 45
EP  - 73
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85152977427&partnerID=40&md5=e7bc9264585aabb76523917f86a71286
AB  - In the last few years, there has been a growing concern about plastic and plastics consumption, and the constant human and environmental exposure to these materials has raised an alarm. As part of the composition of plastic, phthalates have caused a lot of controversy, with several studies implicating these compounds as harmful to human health. Studies have shown the presence of phthalates in 75-90% of the general population, which can be due to their ubiquity in the environment and to not being covalently attached to plastic, being easily released into the environment. These compounds, that are diesters of phthalic acid, are the most common plasticizers used in a variety of products, from polyvinyl chloride (PVC) to personal care products and even medical and pharmaceutical materials. Due to their endocrine-disrupting properties, phthalates have been associated with several diseases including cancer, diabetes, male reproduction and cardiovascular diseases. There are still few studies on the subject, but evidence shows the negative impact of phthalates on the cardiovascular system. From epidemiological and experimental studies, it has already been described the involvement of phthalates in the development heart failure, arrhythmias, stroke, atherosclerosis, hypertension and diabetes. Nevertheless the mechanisms by which phthalates induce these harmful effects are still unclear. In this chapter, we aim to elucidate and relate all the cardiovascular effects of phthalates described so far, and to suggest the following research approaches. © 2022 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
