TI  - Early Life Nutrition, Epigenetics, and Programming of Later Life
PY  - 2022
T2  - Handbook of Nutraceuticals and Natural Products
SP  - 301
EP  - 362
DO  - 10.1002/9781119746843.ch15
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85147902270&doi=10.1002%2f9781119746843.ch15&partnerID=40&md5=1db4a9deba7bfc279995d6bd4b97efef
AB  - The developmental origin of health and disease (DOHaD) hypothesis suggests that epigenetic marks serve as a memory of exposure in early life to inappropriate environment, and these marks induce long-term changes in gene expression, particularly leading to disease in later life. There is now considerable evidence that nutritional imbalances around the time of conception, during pregnancy and/or in infancy or childhood (all critical periods of development) may alter a child's health trajectory and impact risk for noncommunicable diseases (NCDs) across the child's lifespan. Suboptimal nutrition in early life will predispose individuals to adverse health outcomes, viz., poor growth, obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disorder (CVD), immune diseases, osteoporosis, and neurodevelopmental disorders. The late onset of such diseases in response to earlier transient experiences has led to the suggestion that developmental programming may have an epigenetic component, as epigenetic marks such as DNA methylation or histone tail modifications could provide a persistent memory of earlier nutritional states. Nevertheless, animal evidence suggests that a history of nutritional insults in the parental generation could lead to multigenerational and transgenerational predispositions for obesity and NCDs. In this chapter, recent experimental models and human epidemiological studies providing evidences for the causative links identified between nutrition, epigenetics, and developmental programming that can lead to different and unintended long-term consequences on health and disease are revisited. © 2022 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
