TI  - Exploring New Drug Targets for Type 2 Diabetes: Success, Challenges and Opportunities
PY  - 2022
T2  - Biomedicines
VL  - 10
IS  - 2
C7  - 331
DO  - 10.3390/biomedicines10020331
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85124100228&doi=10.3390%2fbiomedicines10020331&partnerID=40&md5=d8e82837ff8a6811b0981d46aa5579df
AB  - There are substantial shortcomings in the drugs currently available for treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus. The global diabetic crisis has not abated despite the introduction of new types of drugs and targets. Persistent unaddressed patient needs remain a significant factor in the quest for new leads in routine studies. Drug discovery methods in this area have followed developments in the market, contributing to a recent rise in the number of molecules. Nevertheless, troubling developments and fresh challenges are still evident. Recently, metformin, the most widely used first-line drug for diabetes, was found to contain a carcinogenic contaminant known as N-nitroso dimethylamine (NDMA). Therefore, purity and toxicity are also a big challenge for drug discovery and development. Moreover, newer drug classes against SGLT-2 illustrate both progress and difficulties. The same was true previously in the case of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists and dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors. Furthermore, researchers must study the importance of mechanistic characteristics of novel compounds, as well as exposure-related hazardous aspects of current and newly identified protein targets, in order to identify new pharmacological molecules with improved selectivity and specificity. © 2022 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.
