TI  - Maternal Hepatitis: Important Considerations
PY  - 2022
T2  - Infections and Pregnancy
SP  - 151
EP  - 164
DO  - 10.1007/978-981-16-7865-3_12
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85169343075&doi=10.1007%2f978-981-16-7865-3_12&partnerID=40&md5=29702212a15146d18d26aee8791c659a
AB  - Viral hepatitis is considered to be the most common cause of jaundice in pregnancy. The known causes are range of hepatitis infections which includes Hepatitis A, B, C, D, E, and G. The epidemiology, structure, virulence, prevalence, route of transmission, mother-to-child transmission, tendency to chronicity, risk of liver complications, and response to antivirals differ for all. The greatest risk to mother and fetus is seen with hepatitis A and E virus. Clinical presentations are the same for all types and differentiation can only be serology based. The risks to the fetus are the main feature of hepatitis A infection and hepatitis E has both increased maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality. Chronic HBV, HDV, and HCV infections are associated with pregnancy-related complications and also risk of mother-to-child transmission (MTCT). Management is supportive in all except in cases of development of fulminant hepatic failure where termination of pregnancy is warranted. Vaginal delivery and breastfeeding are not contraindicated in any type of viral hepatitis. Multispecialty approach is required in these cases for optimal management with the main aim being prevention of maternal and fetal complications and transfer of infection to the neonate. Pregnancy offers an opportunity to chronically infected patients who are asymptomatic and silent carriers to medical follow-up. This will help is reducing the lifelong morbidity of the patients. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2022.
