VL  - 113
IS  - 1
SP  - 172
EP  - 188
DO  - 10.23736/S0026-4806.21.07207-4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85113777861&doi=10.23736%2fS0026-4806.21.07207-4&partnerID=40&md5=157c3a41ef719eea01eb4a9d9ea1fcb5
AB  - Chronobiology studies the biological rhythms or circadian cycles of living organisms and their adaptation to external changes. Biological rhythms can affect hormone production cycles such as sleep/wake, and nutrition/fasting, but these factors can also alter the circadian rhythm (CR). In recent years, numerous studies have highlighted how feeding times and frequency can influence biological rhythms. Additionally, individuals’ chronotype, working shifts, and food intake can make a deep impact on people’s tendency to develop obesity and metabolic diseases. In this context, a single food and a specific combination of these, can also affect the CR and fasting cycle and consequently body weight and viceversa. The purpose of the review is to propose practical nutritional recommendations to help in resynchronizing the circadian rhythm as a tool in weight control. (Cite this article as: Barrea L, Frias-Toral E, Aprano S, Castellucci B, Pugliese G, Rodriguez-Veintimilla D, et al. The clock diet: a practical nutritional guide to manage obesity through chrononutrition. Minerva Med 2022;113:172-88. DOI: 10.23736/ S0026-4806.21.07207-4) © 2021 EDIZIONI MINERVA MEDICA
