AB  - Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a chronic liver disease characterized by excessive cytoplasmic accumulation of triglycerides in hepatocytes, occurring in the absence of excessive alcohol consumption, and currently affects 25%–30% of the global population. NAFLD is strongly associated with the metabolic syndrome and so commonly develops in patients exhibiting comorbidities such as obesity, type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension and dyslipidaemia. Insulin resistance and lipotoxicity are strong drivers of disease, leading to hepatic inflammation and fibrosis that progresses to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. Disease severity is influenced by an individual’s polygenic background with carriage of specific genetic modifiers determining susceptibility to NAFLD and influencing how the disease progresses. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
