SP  - 79
EP  - 113
DO  - 10.1002/9781119739883.ch5
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85151231257&doi=10.1002%2f9781119739883.ch5&partnerID=40&md5=fd6eb6cfe7e11b6d5c8f07f4cf13399a
AB  - The organic anion transporting polypeptides (OATPs) are a superfamily of plasma membrane transporters for endogenous and xenobiotic compounds. With respect to pharmacokinetics, the OATPs are well-recognized determinants of intestinal absorption, tissue distribution, and clearance by eliminating organs. In this chapter, we will review the molecular characteristics of the OATPs, including structure, transport mechanisms, gene expression, as well as transcriptional, post-transcriptional, and epigenetic regulation. Furthermore, we discuss aspects of OATP substrate and inhibitor specificity with specific attention provided to drug and endogenous biomarker transporter activity probes. In the context of clinical pharmacology, the drug-, food-, and formulation-interactions with OATPs are summarized. In addition, the clinical pharmacogenetics and the roles of various diseases on OATP activity and variable drug responses are reviewed. Lastly, we provide an overview of the current understanding of the physiological and pathophysiological roles of OATPs. © 2022 John Wiley and Sons, Inc.
