TI  - Mechanisms of melatonin in obesity: A review
PY  - 2022
T2  - International Journal of Molecular Sciences
VL  - 23
IS  - 1
C7  - 218
DO  - 10.3390/ijms23010218
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85121721614&doi=10.3390%2fijms23010218&partnerID=40&md5=2c560fd3a88e061abe7f836eb640ec22
AB  - Obesity and its complications have become a prominent global public health problem that severely threatens human health. Melatonin, originally known as an effective antioxidant, is an endogenous hormone found throughout the body that serves various physiological functions. In recent decades, increasing attention has been paid to its unique function in regulating energy metabolism, especially in glucose and lipid metabolism. Accumulating evidence has established the relationship between melatonin and obesity; nevertheless, not all preclinical and clinical evidence indicates the anti-obesity effect of melatonin, which makes it remain to conclude the clinical effect of melatonin in the fight against obesity. In this review, we have summarized the current knowledge of melatonin in regulating obesity-related symptoms, with emphasis on its underlying mechanisms. The role of melatonin in regulating the lipid profile, adipose tissue, oxidative stress, and inflammation, as well as the interactions of melatonin with the circadian rhythm, gut microbiota, sleep disorder, as well as the α7nAChR, the opioidergic system, and exosomes, make melatonin a promising agent to open new avenues in the intervention of obesity. © 2021 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.
