UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85125020558&doi=10.1080%2f10641955.2022.2033763&partnerID=40&md5=a62af5f83c7220175ba10aacfba6ffcb
AB  - Pregnancy–induced hypertension (PIH), preeclampsia (PE), and gestational diabetes (GDM) are common adverse outcomes in pregnancy. Objective: To find out whether preconceptual leptin levels differ in subsequent pregnancy between control vs. GDM and hypertensive pregnancy groups. Materials and Methods: Data was from The Cardiovascular Risk in Young Finns Study and The Medical Birth Register of Finland. Of 293 subjects 71 developed GDM, 27 PIH/PE and 201 were controls. Results: Leptin was higher in GDM (p < 0.0001) and PIH/PE (p = 0.0002) groups compared to control. GDM group was robust to BMI matching (p = 0,0081). Conclusion: Leptin was higher in GDM (p < 0.0001) and PIH/PE (p = 0.0002) groups compared to control. GDM group was robust to BMI matching (p = 0,0081). © 2022 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
