VL  - 13
IS  - 11
SP  - 1584
EP  - 1610
DO  - 10.4254/wjh.v13.i11.1584
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85121536937&doi=10.4254%2fwjh.v13.i11.1584&partnerID=40&md5=a85404d26edfed93f759fa2b635a2e47
AB  - Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a heterogeneous condition with a wide spectrum of clinical presentations and natural history and disease severity. There is also substantial inter-individual variation and variable response to a different therapy. This heterogeneity of NAFLD is in turn influenced by various factors primarily demographic/dietary factors, metabolic status, gut microbiome, genetic predisposition together with epigenetic factors. The differential impact of these factors over a variable period of time influences the clinical phenotype and natural history. Failure to address heterogeneity partly explains the sub-optimal response to current and emerging therapies for fatty liver disease. Consequently, leading experts across the globe have recently suggested a change in nomenclature of NAFLD to metabolic-associated fatty liver disease (MAFLD) which can better reflect current knowledge of heterogeneity and does not exclude concomitant factors for fatty liver disease (e.g. alcohol, viral hepatitis, etc.). Precise identification of disease phenotypes is likely to facilitate clinical trial recruitment and expedite translational research for the development of novel and effective therapies for NAFLD/MAFLD. © 2021. Published by Baishideng Publishing Group Inc. All rights reserved.
