TI  - Epigenetics of Hepatic Insulin Resistance
PY  - 2021
T2  - Frontiers in Endocrinology
VL  - 12
C7  - 681356
DO  - 10.3389/fendo.2021.681356
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85107190605&doi=10.3389%2ffendo.2021.681356&partnerID=40&md5=588943a392caafb3ba3ef0afecd8fe2f
AB  - Insulin resistance (IR) is largely recognized as a unifying feature that underlies metabolic dysfunction. Both lifestyle and genetic factors contribute to IR. Work from recent years has demonstrated that the epigenome may constitute an interface where different signals may converge to promote IR gene expression programs. Here, we review the current knowledge of the role of epigenetics in hepatic IR, focusing on the roles of DNA methylation and histone post-translational modifications. We discuss the broad epigenetic changes observed in the insulin resistant liver and its associated pathophysiological states and leverage on the wealth of ‘omics’ studies performed to discuss efforts in pinpointing specific loci that are disrupted by these changes. We envision that future studies, with increased genomic resolution and larger cohorts, will further the identification of biomarkers of early onset hepatic IR and assist the development of targeted interventions. Furthermore, there is growing evidence to suggest that persistent epigenetic marks may be acquired over prolonged exposure to disease or deleterious exposures, highlighting the need for preventative medicine and long-term lifestyle adjustments to avoid irreversible or long-term alterations in gene expression. © Copyright © 2021 Maude, Sanchez-Cabanillas and Cebola.
