TI  - Non-coding RNA crosstalk with nuclear receptors in liver disease
PY  - 2021
T2  - Biochimica et Biophysica Acta - Molecular Basis of Disease
VL  - 1867
IS  - 5
C7  - 166083
DO  - 10.1016/j.bbadis.2021.166083
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85099960114&doi=10.1016%2fj.bbadis.2021.166083&partnerID=40&md5=3634383cff01d31e2987693f15d9c72e
AB  - The dysregulation of nuclear receptors (NRs) underlies the pathogenesis of a variety of liver disorders. Non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs) are defined as RNA molecules transcribed from DNA but not translated into proteins. MicroRNAs (miRNAs) and long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs) are two types of ncRNAs that have been extensively studied for regulating gene expression during diverse cellular processes. NRs as therapeutic targets in liver disease have been exemplified by the successful application of their pharmacological ligands in clinics. MiRNA-based reagents or drugs are emerging as flagship products in clinical trials. Advancing our understanding of the crosstalk between NRs and ncRNAs is critical to the development of diagnostic and therapeutic strategies. This review summarizes recent findings on the reciprocal regulation between NRs and ncRNAs (mainly on miRNAs and lncRNAs) and their implication in liver pathophysiology, which might be informative to the translational medicine of targeting NRs and ncRNAs in liver disease. © 2021 Elsevier B.V.
