TI  - Epigenetic regulation of processes related to high level of fibroblast growth factor 21 in obese subjects
PY  - 2021
T2  - Genes
VL  - 12
IS  - 2
C7  - 307
SP  - 1
EP  - 18
DO  - 10.3390/genes12020307
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85101877022&doi=10.3390%2fgenes12020307&partnerID=40&md5=6d5a29c405c069ec1a4e252eec6e9483
AB  - We hypothesised that epigenetics may play an important role in mediating fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF21) resistance in obesity. We aimed to evaluate DNA methylation changes and miRNA pattern in obese subjects associated with high serum FGF21 levels. The study included 136 participants with BMI 27–45 kg/m2. Fasting FGF21, glucose, insulin, GIP, lipids, adipokines, miokines and cytokines were measured and compared in high serum FGF21 (n = 68) group to low FGF21 (n = 68) group. Human DNA Methylation Microarrays were analysed in leukocytes from each group (n = 16). Expression of miRNAs was evaluated using quantitative PCR-TLDA. The study identified differentially methylated genes in pathways related to glucose transport, insulin secretion and signalling, lipid transport and cellular metabolism, response to nutrient levels, thermogenesis, browning of adipose tissue and bone mineralisation. Additionally, it detected transcription factor genes regulating FGF21 and fibroblast growth factor receptor and vascular endothelial growth factor receptor pathways regulation. Increased expression of hsa-miR-875-5p and decreased expression of hsa-miR-133a-3p, hsa-miR-185-5p and hsa-miR-200c-3p were found in the group with high serum FGF21. These changes were associated with high FGF21, VEGF and low adiponectin serum levels. Our results point to a significant role of the epigenetic regulation of genes involved in metabolic pathways related to FGF21 action. © 2021 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.
