UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85158919682&doi=10.1007%2f978-3-030-84725-8_21&partnerID=40&md5=844b164dfb8c97ce655eb6e35347de0d
AB  - The pregnancy outcome relies on the appropriate interaction of the mother, placenta, and fetus. This chapter deals with those maternal conditions which have an impact on the development and function of the placenta and thus on fetal development and well-being. This includes inherited conditions as well as those that are acquired including those relating to lifestyle choices. Unfortunately, many studies relating to the maternal conditions are observational and do not include placental pathology. Those with some placental findings include details such as weight but no definite information as to whether this is with or without formalin and with or without membranes or cord attached which will have a significant impact on weight. This applies particularly in multicenter trials where practices may vary between institutions. It is recommended that pathology departments implement databases relating to placental pathology associated with the many and varied maternal conditions and associations that complicate pregnancies. There is also a need to adhere to accepted international recommendations on macroscopic and microscopic reporting to assist with comparability of studies and refining the significance of placental pathologies associated with these conditions for mother and/or baby. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2012, 2022.
