UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85129841528&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-323-85170-1.00005-1&partnerID=40&md5=9cd3b318a63da30d5d2bb70d9bbfa6db
AB  - Diabetes is a chronic disease caused by inherited or acquired deficiency in the production of insulin by the pancreas or the inability of the body to make adequate use of the insulin produced. It is mainly of three types, type 1, type 2, and gestational diabetes. The increasing prevalence of diabetes is a worldwide phenomenon following the continuous growth in urbanization, changes in diet, and the emergence of more sedentary lifestyles. The management of diabetes is insulin dependent and or changes in with lifestyle include diet and exercise. Till to date, administration of exogenous insulin is the only known treatment for T1D and direct microbiota management can influence the development of both T1D and T2D. Human and animal studies have shown that probiotic bacteria can significantly changes the gut microbiota and reverse the diabetes. In this book chapter, we provided the information on the role of probiotics in the alleviation of T1D and T2D by preclinical and clinical investigation. Besides this, the role of probiotics in gestational diabetes, and new-born birth weight, etc. Moreover, the recent research on Akkermansia muciniphila and conclusion, suggestion for future studies and perspectives were provided. © 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
