TI  - Metabolomics in bio-medical research: Challenges and opportunities
PY  - 2021
T2  - Advances in Medicine and Biology. Volume 182
SP  - 175
EP  - 208
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85132956852&partnerID=40&md5=60781c94eec502b3b377f74c14be25a9
AB  - The role of lipopolysaccharides (LPS) released from Gram-negative bacteria in the disease sepsis that is responsible for many deaths worldwide is well-known. The need for the detection of LPS has motivated the development of many biosensors and assay methods beginning with those based on early classical approaches such as the rabbit pyrogen test, the monocyte activation test, and the Limulus-based test methods. Subsequently, modern methods based on binding to proteins, antibodies, or aptamers with various spectroscopic detection schemes were developed. More recently, approaches using nanomaterials including gold nanoparticles, quantum dots, nanotubes, and magnetic particles and new approaches such as surface plasmon resonance, electrochemical detection, and molecular imprinting have been introduced. The process of LPS binding to cells is a complex multi-step process involving a sequence of binding partners including LPS binding protein (LBP), cluster of differentiation 14 (CD14) in both its membranebound and soluble forms, membrane protein MD-2 also known as myeloid differentiation factor 2 or lymphocyte antigen 96, and toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) with the pathways that are activated ultimately leading to expression of pro-inflammatory cytokines. Whether LPS is present in monomeric or aggregated form also influences these binding events. Assays that aim at unraveling these binding interactions are of great significance and are herein reviewed. There have also been attempts to interfere with these binding interactions through the introduction of synthetic analogs of the lipid A portion of LPS and efforts to use assays to evaluate the effectiveness of such analogs are reviewed. © 2021 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
