TI  - Mitochondrial dysfunction in metabolic disorders
PY  - 2021
T2  - Mitochondrial Dysfunction and Nanotherapeutics: Aging, Diseases, and Nanotechnology-Related Strategies in Mitochondrial Medicine
SP  - 91
EP  - 137
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-323-85666-9.00015-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85129011801&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-323-85666-9.00015-2&partnerID=40&md5=3e26408ecd6a706e044050f643923288
AB  - Mitochondrial dysfunction initiates a cascade of events that ultimately leads to metabolic syndromes (Met S), obesity, Type 2 diabetes, nonalcoholic liver cirrhosis. The cumulative impact of dysfunctions, for instance, oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) and generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) enhance the progression of Alzheimer’s disease (AD), Parkinson’s disease (PD), and other neurodegenerative diseases (NDDs). However, mitochondrial dysfunction due to electron transport chain (ETC) disruption and impaired adenosine triphosphate (ATP) production has been linked to insulin resistance (IR) through deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) damage, oxidative stress, and PI3K-Akt-GSKβ pathway. Thus, therapies targeting mitochondrial dysfunction, including biomolecules and drug administration, central nervous system (CNS)-based therapies, and heat shock proteins (HSP)-mediated rescue have been considered to combat mitochondrial dysfunction-induced ailments. Herein, we discuss clearance pathways, IR, DNA damage response, and signaling pathways associated with metabolic disease and NDDs. We further discussed CNS associated transcription factor peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors (PPARs) in the regulation of mitochondrial dysfunction followed by therapeutics. © 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
