UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85126406213&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-814118-2.00016-1&partnerID=40&md5=bf827172855368c97597f6e42ded73c5
AB  - Energy demand in heart and skeletal muscle changes enormously in response to physical exercise. Commensurate with altered energy demand cellular energy supply has to adapt which requires regulatory elements. Energy is mainly generated via mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation by the respiratory chain. Mechanical muscle twitching is mediated by cytosolic calcium transients which translate into mitochondrial calcium transients and are prone to regulate energy metabolism. Calcium can upregulate enzymes of the citric acid cycle, fatty acid oxidation, and respiratory chain, all located in mitochondria. Furthermore, sirtuins are important regulators of energy metabolism; especially sirtuins 1 and 3 are upregulated during exercise. Sirtuin 1 has a positive effect on mitochondrial biogenesis, thus increasing the capacity of mitochondrial energy supply. Furthermore, sirtuin 3 upregulates different intramitochondrial enzymes, thus increasing energy supply, NAD+ (which accumulates during exercise), as a substrate of sirtuins, is able to upregulate sirtuin flux in a feed-forward manner. There is also exercise-dependent upregulation of NAD+ synthesis. Sirtuin 4 regulates long-chain fatty acid oxidation by inhibiting malonyl CoA carboxylase. Elevated levels of malonyl CoA inhibit mitochondrial uptake of long-chain fatty acids. © 2021 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
