TI  - Visfatin: A possible role in cardiovasculo-metabolic disorders
PY  - 2020
T2  - Cells
VL  - 9
IS  - 11
C7  - 2444
SP  - 1
EP  - 19
DO  - 10.3390/cells9112444
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85096083087&doi=10.3390%2fcells9112444&partnerID=40&md5=421adb4f1f491f5503d20538c2738aaf
AB  - Visfatin/NAMPT (nicotinamide phosphoribosyltransferase) is an adipocytokine with several intriguing properties. It was first identified as pre-B-cell colony-enhancing factor but turned out to possess enzymatic functions in nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide biosynthesis, with ubiquitous expression in skeletal muscles, liver, cardiomyocytes, and brain cells. Visfatin exists in an intracellular (iNAMPT) and extracellular (eNAMPT) form. Intracellularly, visfatin/iNAMPT plays a regulatory role in NAD+ biosynthesis and thereby affects many NAD-dependent proteins such as sirtuins, PARPs, MARTs and CD38/157. Extracellularly, visfatin is associated with many hormone-like signaling pathways and activates some intracellular signaling cascades. Importantly, eNAMPT has been associated with several metabolic disorders including obesity and type 1 and 2 diabetes. In this review, a brief overview about visfatin is presented with special emphasis on its relevance to metabolic diseases. Visfatin/NAMPT appears to be a unique molecule with clinical significance with a prospective promising diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic applications in many cardiovasculo-metabolic disorders. © 2020 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.
