TI  - Triglycerides and the risk of developing metabolic glucose disorders
PY  - 2020
T2  - Triglycerides: An Overview
SP  - 53
EP  - 72
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85146193809&partnerID=40&md5=48f2a6b88f5e7c9aeaab1a46200c6ff3
AB  - On the basis of available experimental and clinical evidence, the elevated plasma triglycerides levels seem to play an important role in the dysregulation of glucose homeostasis. With this regard, it has been reported that the increase of fatty acid oxidation, related to hypertriglyceridemia, inhibit several steps of glycolytic oxidation pathway and decreases of glucose transport into cell; phenomenon that results in the decrease of insulin sensitivity in the skeletal muscle that leads to hyperglycemia. Based on the close relationship between elevated triglycerides and insulin resistance, recently, we developed the triglycerides-to-glucose index as a biomarker for identifying insulin resistance. Furthermore, results of studies in adults showed that hypertriglyceridemia is an independent risk factor for the development of hyperglycemia, suggesting that elevated triglyceride levels may precede the appearance of glucose metabolic disorders. The above mentioned strongly suggests that hypertriglyceridemia may play an important role in the pathogenesis of insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes. Hence, in this chapter we review available data in order to evaluate the usefulness of the triglyceride-to-glucose index, and whether triglycerides per se directly contribute to the development of metabolic glucose disorders. © 2020 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
