TI  - Probiotic and omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids supplementation reduces insulin resistance, improves glycemia and obesity parameters in individuals with type 2 diabetes: A randomised controlled trial
PY  - 2020
T2  - Obesity Medicine
VL  - 19
C7  - 100248
DO  - 10.1016/j.obmed.2020.100248
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85084442764&doi=10.1016%2fj.obmed.2020.100248&partnerID=40&md5=8885b0a9fcebfc530c9ce52733d4ea03
AB  - Background: Probiotics and omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids supplementation (PUFA) have beneficial effect on obesity related disorders in animal models. Methods: A total of 54 patients met the criteria and were included in double-blind single centre RCT, to receive “Symbiter-Omega” (combination of multiprobiotics with omega-3 PUFA) or placebo for 8-weeks administered as a sachet formulation. The primary main outcome was the change HOMA2-IR. Results: Combined use of the probiotic mixture with omega-3 PUFA leads to a significant reduction of HOMA2-IR (р = 0.018) and improvement of insulin sensitivity (% S) (p = 0.010) after 8 weeks of treatment period. Simultaneously were detected lowering of HbA1c from 8.26 ± 0.82 to 7.80 ± 0.86% (р = 0.006). In secondary outcome analysis significant decrease of body weight, BMI and markers of chronic systemic inflammation in comparison with placebo were found. Conclusion: Probiotic combination with omega-3 PUFA modestly improved insulin resistance and obesity related parameters in patients with type 2 diabetes. © 2020 Elsevier Ltd
