TI  - Biologic conditions for bone growth and maintenance: Managing the oxidative stress
PY  - 2020
T2  - Bone Augmentation by Anatomical Region: Techniques and Decision-Making
SP  - 17
EP  - 41
DO  - 10.1002/9781119427926.ch2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85159834786&doi=10.1002%2f9781119427926.ch2&partnerID=40&md5=d3bcacfd8145decc91e60d155e6e3d4e
AB  - This chapter highlights the relationship between biologic conditions, oxidative stress and long-term stability in bone management. Cholesterol and vitamin D have a major influence on bone and tissue metabolism. Anxiety is a major oxidative stress factor. The easiest way to decrease this stress and its effects on surgical outcomes is to perform pre and per-operative sedation, oral, or IV. Implants are placed in native or grafted bone. In case of tissue damage, inflammation and/or infection may secondarily develop and results in peri-implantitis. These complications have given rise to numerous theories, such as biomaterials resorbtion, inadequacy of protocols or the influence of a concomitant periodontal disease. Platelet rich fibrin (PRF) (leukocyte-PRF or L-PRF) additionally contains white blood cells; necessary cells involved in the wound healing process by improving defense immunity and secreting a large quantity of growth factors. © 2020 John Wiley & Sons Ltd. All rights reserved.
