TI  - Endocrine-disrupting chemicals and insulin resistance in children
PY  - 2020
T2  - Biomedicines
VL  - 8
IS  - 6
C7  - 137
DO  - 10.3390/BIOMEDICINES8060137
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85087087924&doi=10.3390%2fBIOMEDICINES8060137&partnerID=40&md5=ad937e02226884ad2e4636fad493a6e2
AB  - The purpose of this article is to review the evidence linking background exposure to endocrine-disrupting chemicals (EDCs) with insulin resistance in children. Although evidence in children is scarce since very few prospective studies exist even in adults, evidence that EDCs might be involved in the development of insulin resistance and related diseases such as obesity and diabetes is accumulating. We reviewed the literature on both cross-sectional and prospective studies in humans and experimental studies. Epidemiological studies show a statistical link between exposure to pesticides, polychlorinated bisphenyls, bisphenol A, phthalates, aromatic polycyclic hydrocarbides, or dioxins and insulin resistance. © 2020 by the authors.
