TI  - Maternal Diabetes Mellitus and Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension of the Newborn: Accumulated Evidence From Observational Studies
PY  - 2020
T2  - Canadian Journal of Diabetes
VL  - 44
IS  - 4
SP  - 327
EP  - 334.e3
DO  - 10.1016/j.jcjd.2019.10.002
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85077355385&doi=10.1016%2fj.jcjd.2019.10.002&partnerID=40&md5=89807d0946f09c2771e517485a1e4e71
AB  - Objectives: Maternal diabetes mellitus (including pre-existing and gestational diabetes mellitus) is linked with adverse infant outcomes. However, the question of whether maternal diabetes increases the risk of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) is unclear. Herein, we conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis to summarize clinical evidence to determine the association between maternal diabetes mellitus and PPHN. Methods: In this systematic review and meta-analysis, we systematically searched PubMed, Embase, Cochrane Library, Web of Science and Google Scholar to identify relevant studies according to predefined criteria. Data from selected studies were extracted, and meta-analysis was performed using fixed effects modelling. Results: In all, we included 7 unique studies with aggregated data on 2 million individuals and >5,000 cases of PPHN. Maternal diabetes was significantly associated with a higher risk of PPHN (risk ratio [RR], 1.37; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.23 to 1.51). Both case-control and cohort studies exhibited that the presence of maternal diabetes increased the risk of PPHN (case-control: RR, 1.91; 95% CI, 1.02 to 2.79; cohort: RR, 1.36; 95% CI, 1.22 to 1.50). By omitting 1 study at a time, sensitivity analysis made sure that no individual study was entirely responsible for the combined results. Conclusions: Maternal diabetes was associated with increased risk of PPHN. For babies with refractory hypoxemia, with mothers with diabetes, PPHN should be taken into consideration in clinical practice. © 2019 The Authors
