TI  - HYPERGLYCEMIA IN PREGNANCY AMONG SOUTH ASIAN WOMEN: EXPERIENCE FROM UNIVERSAL SCREENING AND MULTI-DISCIPLINARY MANAGEMENT
PY  - 2020
T2  - Gestational Diabetes: From Diagnosis to Treatment
SP  - 233
EP  - 255
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85148825711&partnerID=40&md5=4b006f68c7e320011b4531ac065b63f4
AB  - Estimates indicate that South Asia accounts for over two fifths of the global burden of hyperglycemia in pregnancy (HIP). Considering the high risk for women of South Asian descent, universal screening for HIP is recommended. Optimal glycemic control preconception and throughout pregnancy improves pregnancy outcome. Abnormal glucose tolerance during pregnancy results in short- and long-term adverse outcomes for mother, fetus and offspring. Pregnancy is an excellent opportunity to recognize and institute preventive strategies to achieve primary and secondary prevention for the mothers and primordial prevention for the next generation. Multi-disciplinary and a life course approach with a holistic outlook in managing these women are advocated by many professional groups in South Asia. However, there are numerous challenges and barriers in managing these women. This chapter addresses background, pathophysiology, and impact of HIP, screening, diagnosis and multi-disciplinary management from preconception to postpartum follow up. It also describes the challenges and barriers in managing these women. © 2020 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
