TI  - Structure-activity relationship between food components and metabolic syndrome
PY  - 2020
T2  - Food Structure and Functionality
SP  - 235
EP  - 248
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-821453-4.00001-6
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85140663393&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-821453-4.00001-6&partnerID=40&md5=c38b854418cec2a5b6031a9937a58104
AB  - The various components of food proteins, such as fats, carbohydrates, dietary fiber, vitamins, minerals, and water are needed for the optimal performance of various organs of the body. Although each component of food has some particular function, all these components interact with each other so that their utilization and functions are optimal in such a way that health is maintained and recovery from diseases is quick and smooth. Each component of food seems to have an essential and specific role in the maintenance of immune response, and their deficiencies/imbalance may result in the development of metabolic syndrome, cancer, and other diseases. In this chapter, the role of various components of food in the pathobiology of metabolic syndrome is highlighted with particular attention to their role in the metabolism of essential fatty acids, an essential but often ignored component of food, and its impact on metabolic syndrome and cancer. © 2021 Elsevier Inc.
