TI  - Folate, vitamin B12, and choline: Essential nutrients before and during pregnancy and during lactation
ST  - Folat, Vitamin B12 und Cholin: Essenzielle Nährstoffe vor und während der Schwangerschaft und in der Stillzeit
PY  - 2020
T2  - Gynakologische Praxis
VL  - 46
IS  - 4
SP  - 607
EP  - 617
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85091663966&partnerID=40&md5=778e420727c4b09eca11817e5afc748b
AB  - Folate supplementation before and during pregnancy can prevent many birth defects such as spina bifida. The prevention of such defects on a population level is effective and aims at increasing folate intake in as many women as possible before they become pregnant. However, there are many anomalies that cannot be prevented by folate supplementation. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes disturbance in folate metabolism and utilization. Women who adhere to a vegan or a vegetarian diet could develop vitamin B12 deficiency and thereby could have higher risk for early abortion, preterm birth, low birth weight, and spina bifida despite that they have high intake of folate through the diet or supplements. In addition to folate, choline is a methyl donor that plays a significant role during the pregnancy, during the prenatal life, and for child development after birth. Folate and choline could show complementary and synergetic effects on the health of the mother and the child. Choline requirements increase during pregnancy and lactation. The diet and the estrogen-induced endogenous synthesis of choline in maternal liver are not sufficient to fulfill the needs in the pregnancy. The dietary intake of choline is often not sufficient during pregnancy. It is currently unclear whether low choline status during early pregnancy is associated with the risk of neural tube defects or congenital heart diseases. Results of upcoming studies are awaited. © 2020 Mediengruppe Oberfranken - Fachverlage GmbH & Co. KG. All rights reserved.
