TI  - The therapeutic potential of metformin in breast and ovarian cancer
PY  - 2019
T2  - The Pharmacological Guide to Metformin
SP  - 157
EP  - 219
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85156178439&partnerID=40&md5=8ab108b8f21f5416bcd5a07ea870657f
AB  - Metformin is the first-line drug used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes with more than six decades of clinical use. It is a relatively safe drug, with known pharmacokinetics and a favorable safety profile. In recent years, in addition to hypoglycemic effect, several epidemiological studies have highlighted the therapeutic potential of metformin in the context of other diseases, especially in breast and ovarian cancers; thus, demonstrating its beneficial actions on survival outcome and prevention of these highly lethal malignancies. Recent and robust data support the fact that metformin would provide invaluable benefits when administrated along with conventional adjuvant therapy against the breast and ovarian cancers. Metformin impairs cellular metabolism, suppresses oncogenic signaling pathways, inhibits cell growth and metastasis, as well as reverses chemoresistance. Thus, improving treatment efficacy and allowing the prescription of lower doses of highly toxic antineoplastic agents. Altogether, the aforementioned information points out metformin as an anticancer agent. Of clinical interest, it arises as a therapeutic possibility in the fight against poor prognosis diseases, such as triplenegative breast cancer and high grade serous ovarian cancer. In the present chapter, we summarized the molecular mechanisms underlying the anticancer effects of metformin in breast and ovarian cancers. Furthermore, we discuss the potential anticancer applications of metformin-based combinatorial chemotherapy. © 2020 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
