TI  - Pathoepigenetics: The Role of Epigenetic Biomarkers in Disease Pathogenesis
PY  - 2019
T2  - Pharmacoepigenetics
SP  - 139
EP  - 189
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-813939-4.00005-X
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85123668257&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-813939-4.00005-X&partnerID=40&md5=5806a2eaef7ed156794d29d776816483
AB  - Pathoepigenetics refers to the role that alterations in the epigenetic machinery exert on human disease. Over 80% of complex disorders (cardiovascular, cerebral, metabolic, inflammatory, neoplastic) result from the interaction of genomic, epigenetic, and environmental factors, which induce a cascade of pathogenic events leading to a particular phenotype. Epigenetic aberrations involving DNA methylation, chromatin remodeling, histone posttranslational modifications and miRNA regulation contribute to the development of pathogenic phenotypes. Mutations in genes encoding elements of the epigenetic network cause epigenetic Mendelian disorders; primary epigenetic changes may be pathogenic, and secondary epigenetic changes may be the consequence of exogenous events and collateral marks of the disease. For the past few years, multiple epigenetic signatures have been associated with cancer, cardiovascular disorders, metabolic disorders, brain disorders, and diverse human pathologies. In the near future these epigenetic biomarkers will be incorporated in medical practice for optimizing predictive (presymptomatic) diagnosis, precision (symptomatic) diagnosis, drug effect (efficacy and safety) monitoring, and personalized treatment (pharmacoepigenetics). © 2019 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
