TI  - Folate, vitamin B12, and choline: Essential nutrients before and during pregnancy and during lactation
ST  - Folat, vitamin B12 und cholin: Essentielle nährstoffe vor und während der schwangerschaft und in der stillzeit
PY  - 2019
T2  - Padiatrische Praxis
VL  - 93
IS  - 1
SP  - 26
EP  - 36
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85091599007&partnerID=40&md5=7523f47652824cbbff8b4e092e89c138
AB  - Folate supplementation before and during pregnancy can prevent many birth defects such as spina bifida. The prevention of such defects on a population level is effective and aims at increasing folate intake in as many women as possible before they become pregnant. However, there are many anomalies that cannot be prevented by folate supplementation. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes disturbance in folate metabolism and utilization. Women who adhere to a vegan or a vegetarian diet could develop vitamin B12 deficiency and thereby could have higher risk for early abortion, preterm birth, low birth weight, and spina bifida despite that they have high intake of folate through the diet or supplements. In addition to folate, choline is a methyl donor that plays a significant role during the pregnancy, during the prenatal life, and for child development after birth. Folate and choline could show complementary and synergetic effects on the health or the mother and the child. Choline requirements increase during pregnancy and lactation. The diet and the estrogen-induced endogenous synthesis of choline in maternal liver are not sufficient to fulfill the needs in the pregnancy. The dietary intake of choline is often not sufficient during pregnancy. It is currently unclear whether low choline status during early pregnancy is associated with the risk of neural tube defects or congenital heart diseases. Results of upcoming studies are awaited. © 2019 Mediengruppe Oberfranken - Fachverlage GmbH & Co. KG. All rights reserved.
