AB  - Over the past 50years, the prevalence of pediatric obesity has increased to epidemic proportions across the globe. The body mass index (BMI) can be used to assess obesity in children, but according to age and sex-specific BMI percentiles, due to natural increases in height and weight that occur during childhood. The rise in pediatric obesity is multifactorial, including changes in dietary, agricultural, and activity practices. Genetic predisposition also increases risk of developing obesity, but can in part be mediated by socioeconomic factors. Prevention efforts have mainly focused on family, school or community-based interventions to promote healthy lifestyle behaviors and increasingly, on public policy changes to reduce risk factors such as sugar sweetened beverage consumption and unhealthy food marketing targeting children and families. © 2020 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
