TI  - Notch signaling dynamically regulates adult β cell proliferation and maturity
PY  - 2019
T2  - Journal of Clinical Investigation
VL  - 129
IS  - 1
C7  - CI98098
DO  - 10.1172/JCI98098
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85059383626&doi=10.1172%2fJCI98098&partnerID=40&md5=c6e22215081494103f6da21a68b19a59
AB  - Notch signaling regulates differentiation of the pancreatic endocrine lineage during embryogenesis, but the role of Notch in mature β cells is unclear. We found that islets derived from lean mice show modest β cell Notch activity, which increases in obesity and in response to high glucose. This response appeared maladaptive, as mice with β cell-specific-deficient Notch transcriptional activity showed improved glucose tolerance when subjected to high-fat diet feeding. Conversely, mice with β cell-specific Notch gain of function (β-NICD) had a progressive loss of β cell maturity, due to proteasomal degradation of MafA, leading to impaired glucose-stimulated insulin secretion and glucose intolerance with aging or obesity. Surprisingly, Notch-active β cells had increased proliferative capacity, leading to increased but dysfunctional β cell mass. These studies demonstrate a dynamic role for Notch in developed β cells for simultaneously regulating β cell function and proliferation. © 2019 American Society for Clinical Investigation. All rights reserved.
