TI  - A thorough analysis of diabetes research in China from 1995 to 2015: current scenario and future scope
PY  - 2019
T2  - Science China Life Sciences
VL  - 62
IS  - 1
SP  - 46
EP  - 62
DO  - 10.1007/s11427-018-9377-y
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85054317653&doi=10.1007%2fs11427-018-9377-y&partnerID=40&md5=1fa85319e6b84dfb185464d30e0f4dab
AB  - The prevalence of diabetes has increased dramatically over the past three decades, and currently, China has the largest number of diabetics worldwide; this number continues to grow and puts ongoing strains on the medical resources. In this review, we reviewed the diabetes research conducted in China from 1995 to 2015 with the aim of providing new insights regarding the current status and future perspectives for researchers, diabetes health providers, and respective policy-makers. Remarkable progress has been made in diabetes research in China during the past two decades in terms of both the quantity and publication influence. The progress, however, struggles to adequately manage diabetes in China. Here we addressed opportunities to strengthen researches, including new drug development, high quality studies on health economics, and healthcare quality improvement studies. As the expected wave of diabetic complications is upcoming and overwhelming, we therefore recommend that immediate improvements are required to implement the researches regarding their prevention and treatment. © 2018, Science China Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature.
