AB  - Over the past decades, the perinatal period has been shown to play a pivotal role for life-long health. Multiple influencing factors before and during pregnancy as well as during early stages of postnatal development can influence organogenesis and long-term organ function. Among them, prenatal maternal nutrition, psychosocial stress and immunological processes range among the most intensively studied. But even parental health before conception has been reported to influence the offspring's long-term health. In search for the underlying mechanisms, epigenetic processes have been identified to influence long-term disease susceptibility and even transduction of disease risk to future generations. The perinatal period should thus be seen as a window of opportunity for taking preventive measures and improving the health of generations to come. A growing awareness among physicians for the vulnerability and potential of the perinatal period will majorly contribute to the development of effective preventive measures. © 2018 by Mediengruppe Oberfranken - Fachverlage GmbH & Co. KG.
