AB  - The study of the human microbiome has experienced marked growth, enabled by developments in genomic sequencing technology. Growing evidence suggests that the gastrointestinal microbiome likely has important effects on human health and disease risk. Dietary factors such as probiotics, prebiotic carbohydrates, and other dietary compounds appear to be important in influencing the composition of the gut microbiome. Comparisons of geographically distinct populations with substantially different diets have demonstrated quite different fecal microbiome compositions. It is expected that vegetarian diets may have important effects on the gut microbiome, and consequently on health, although the existing published evidence is limited and somewhat inconsistent. © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
