AB  - Obesity is a worldwide epidemic with an alarming high prevalence. Accompanying this rise, there is an increase in the comorbidities such as metabolic syndrome, bone metabolism complications and possible presence of sleep disorders. It is known that lifestyle modifications, such as, regular practice of physical exercises and healthy eating habits incorporation could result beneficially many metabolic health biomarkers, such as improvement in lipid profile, visceral fat deposit and insulin resistance. Mainly for exercise training in obesity control, many weight loss programs are based in the aerobic training intervention. However, some investigations demonstrated that resistance training can improve muscle mass, favoring weight loss process and controlling obesity and it's comorbidities. In fact, resistance exercise is able to increase excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC) and consequently the resting energy expenditure, favoring the weight loss. Moreover evidence suggests that the increase on muscle mass may promote beneficial effects on production and action of miokines, glucose and lipid metabolism. Recently, studies indicated that the combination of aerobic and resistance training with other interdisciplinary interventions is more effective than aerobic exercise alone to improve the health of people with obesity, suggesting as an important non-pharmacological strategy to be applied in the control and treatment of obesity. © 2016 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
