TI  - Fish and fish oil and the metabolic syndrome
PY  - 2016
T2  - Fish and Fish Oil in Health and Disease Prevention
SP  - 169
EP  - 177
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-802844-5.00014-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85018825276&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-802844-5.00014-2&partnerID=40&md5=bda62fe428be05d54915becca55ebd57
AB  - Metabolic syndrome is a condition in which multiple metabolic risk factors for cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes are present (abdominal obesity, dyslipidemia, hyperglycemia, and hypertension). The impact of metabolic syndrome on public health is profound through the increased risk of morbidity and mortality. Fish consumption may improve metabolic health and have a protective role in the prevention of metabolic syndrome. This protective role may however be related to gender, as men may benefit more from the consumption of fish. Both fish and fish oil are beneficial in reducing the metabolic syndrome through their ability to modulating lipid metabolism and to affect inflammatory processes. Further, both lean and fatty fish may have a protective role through positive effects on satiety and to provision of a variety of different nutrients. Additional research is required to further explore these associations to improve or reverse metabolic syndrome and its components. © 2016 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
