TI  - Effects of choline on DNA methylation and macronutrient metabolic gene expression in in vitro models of hyperglycemia
PY  - 2016
T2  - Nutrition and Metabolic Insights
VL  - 9
SP  - 11
EP  - 17
DO  - 10.4137/NMI.S29465
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85015503816&doi=10.4137%2fNMI.S29465&partnerID=40&md5=1fd21ed2164390f0b5ee2e3e932ad50f
AB  - Choline is an essential nutrient that plays an important role in lipid metabolism and DNA methylation. Studies in rodents suggest that choline may adversely affect glycemic control, yet studies in humans are lacking. Using the human hepatic and placental cells, HepG2 and BeWo, respectively, we examined the interaction between choline and glucose treatments. In HepG2 cells, choline supplementation (1 mM) increased global DNA methylation and DNA methyltransferase expression in both low-glucose (5 mM) and high-glucose (35 mM) conditions. Choline supplementation increased the expression of peroxisomal acyl-coenzyme A oxidase 1 (ACOX1), which mediates fatty acid β-oxidation, especially in the high-glucose condition. Highglucose exposure increased the transcription of the gluconeogenic gene phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK), while choline supplementation mitigated such increase. Compared to HepG2 cells, the placenta-derived BeWo cells were relatively unresponsive to either high-glucose or -choline treatment. In conclusion, choline and glucose interacted to affect macronutrient metabolic genes, yet there was no indication that choline may worsen glycemic control in these in vitro human cell culture models. © the authors, publisher and licensee Libertas Academica Limited.
