VL  - 24
IS  - 4
SP  - 174
EP  - 180
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85014871373&partnerID=40&md5=cfc7159f588346723c1d472c1fd48f86
AB  - Metabolic status is undoubtedly changed, latently or manifestly, with the presence of most pathological conditions. In addition, metabolic syndrome as a state of metabolic dysregulation can have a significant importance for pathologies arising in various organs, such as ovaries and liver. Insulin resistance, a hallmark of metabolic syndrome, leads to a higher levels of circulating insulin which induces steroidogenesis in the ovary. A state of hyperandrogenism is achieved and overt symptoms such as acne and hirsutism are seen, but the metabolic shift happening in the body is also taking its toll. Liver, among all the reactive changes, produces less sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). The shortage of SHBG is a definite part of the vicious cycle involving insulin and hyperandrogenemia since it represents the main carrier protein for testosterone. Lower levels of SHBG and higher level of insulin resistance has been marked in pathologies such as polycystic ovarian syndrome, metabolic syndrome, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease and type 2 diabetes mellitus. Elevated BMI, visceral adiposity and inflammation are associated with lower SHBG concentrations, while physical fitness and exercise can increase concentrations of SHBG as well as estrogens. Higher levels of SHBG have been shown to correlate with a healthy hormonal status and reduce the risks for development of cardiovascular diseases. Novel research brought findings that a unique G-protein coupled receptor for SHBG exists. This already proved to be important in the field of oncology where breast cancer cells respond differently to estrogen when SHBG is also bound to its receptor. Future focus on the active role of sex-steroid carrier proteins might upgrade the diagnostic process, treatment follow-up and therapy. This review demonstrates the interconnection between insulin and SHBG in common diseases affecting the female population. © 2015, Hrvatsko Drustvo Ginekologa i Opstetricara. All rights reserved.
