TI  - Critical importance of the perinatal period in the development of obesity
PY  - 2014
T2  - Treatment of the Obese Patient
SP  - 57
EP  - 71
DO  - 10.1007/978-1-4939-1203-2_4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84929929691&doi=10.1007%2f978-1-4939-1203-2_4&partnerID=40&md5=6b3ad269ed68a6a9c4ca29e1a1417c42
AB  - The intrauterine environment is increasingly recognized as an important contributor to the rapidly rising incidence of juvenile obesity and associated metabolic disorders. Clinical and epidemiological studies have repeatedly shown a strong association between maternal BMI and childhood obesity and co-morbidities in the offspring. However not all obese women give birth to overweight infants and the intrauterine effects resulting from maternal obesity that trigger metabolic disease in the offspring are not well understood. Here we discuss the consequences of excess maternal fuels, inflammation, and oxidative stress associated with maternal obesity and insulin resistance, and how these may combine to affect fetal development on a cellular and molecular level to change appetite, mitochondria, and fuel storage. While reducing excess gestational weight gain or treatment of gestational diabetes may improve maternal cardiometabolic parameters in obese women during pregnancy, very little research has been done to examine long-term outcomes in the infant or children. Likewise, dietary interventions in the critical first year of life when infants triple their body fat may impact the microbiome, appetite regulatory control systems, and adipose stem cell biology and also need further study. Direct modification of epigenetic changes may also be a powerful treatment for obese mothers. It is clear that epigenetic regulation, specifically via methylation and acetylation are dynamic processes that change over time. All of these time points are pivotal for breaking the cycle of maternal to infant obesity, but more studies are needed to support recommending specific treatments that could halt the transgenerational cycle of obesity. © 2014 Springer Science+Business Media New York. All rights are reserved.
