TI  - A comparison of the effectiveness of physical activity and sedentary behaviour interventions in reducing sedentary time in adults: A systematic review and meta-analysis of controlled trials
PY  - 2014
T2  - Obesity Reviews
VL  - 15
IS  - 11
SP  - 905
EP  - 919
DO  - 10.1111/obr.12215
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84926159211&doi=10.1111%2fobr.12215&partnerID=40&md5=0977c2a99023f59bf0656059068a7df8
AB  - The objective of this study was to systematically review the literature and compare the effectiveness of controlled interventions with a focus on physical activity (PA) and/or sedentary behaviours (SBs) for reducing sedentary time in adults. Six electronic databases were searched to identify all studies that examined the effects of interventions that targeted PA and/or SBs and that reported on changes in SBs (sedentary, sitting or television time). A qualitative synthesis was performed for all studies, and meta-analyses conducted among studies with mean differences (min/d) of sedentary time. PROSPERO: CRD42014006535. Sixty-five controlled studies met inclusion criteria; 33 were used in the meta-analyses. Interventions with a focus on PA or that included a PA and SB component produced less consistent findings and generally resulted in modest reductions in sedentary time (PA: standardized mean differences [SMD]=-0.22 [95% confidence interval {CI}:-0.35,-0.10], PA+SB: SMD=-0.37 [95% CI:-0.69,-0.05]). Moderate quality evidence from the randomized controlled trial meta-analysis coupled with the qualitative synthesis provides consistent evidence that large and clinically meaningful reductions in sedentary time can be expected from interventions with a focus on reducing SBs (SMD=-1.28 [95% CI:-1.68,-0.87]). There is evidence to support the need for interventions to include a component focused on reducing SBs in order to generate clinically meaningful reductions in sedentary time. © 2014 The Authors. Obesity Reviews published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of World Obesity.
