TI  - Normal Adipose Tissue Biology: Adipocytokines and Inflammation
PY  - 2014
T2  - Pathobiology of Human Disease: A Dynamic Encyclopedia of Disease Mechanisms
SP  - 488
EP  - 497
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-386456-7.02006-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85043260627&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-386456-7.02006-2&partnerID=40&md5=fa859d2c752503c163c98c150fa30ff9
AB  - The adipose tissue is now considered an active endocrine organ. Adipocytes and immune cells (mainly macrophages) in the adipose tissue secrete biologically active proteins that act locally or at distant sites and thus regulate metaboLism. These proteins are collectively referred to as adipocytokines. As obesity develops, adipocytes enlarge and die while macrophages massively infiltrate the adipose tissue. As a result, the adipocytokine profile shifts from anti-inflammatory to proinflammatory. Leptin, tumor necrosis factor, and interleukin-6 are well-characterized proinflammatory adipocytokines. Adiponectin is an anti-inflammatory and insuLin-sensitizing adipocytokine. Proinflammatory adipocytokines generally increase, while anti-inflammatory adipocytokines decrease in obesity and these changes mediate development of obesity-related metaboLic dysfunctions such as insuLin resistance, dysLipidemia, nonalcohoLic fatty Liver disease, and atherosclerosis. However, therapeutic interventions targeting adipocytokines have not been very successful in the treatment of obesity and associated metaboLic conditions. More work on the detailed action of adipocytokines will not only help understand the pathophysiology of metaboLic dysfunction associated with obesity but also assist to identify therapeutic targets to prevent or reverse these metaboLic derangements. © 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
