AU  - Shirai, K.
TI  - Serum lipoprotein lipase mass as a novel biomarker of insulin sensitivity: A role in the evaluation of lipid-and glucose-targeting drugs in adipocytes andskeletal muscle cells
PY  - 2013
T2  - Triglycerides: Chemical Structure, Biosynthesis and Role in Disease
SP  - 123
EP  - 145
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84895204920&partnerID=40&md5=190bd708488845c1056861c9b48c7aab
AB  - Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) plays an important role in the absorption of TG from the bloodstream into the tissues. LPL abnormalities cause hypertriglyceridemia and low high-density lipoprotein (HDL)-cholesterol levels. LPL mass is usually measured after a heparin injection (postheparin LPL mass). LPL mass was found to exist in the serum before heparin injection (preheparin serum). The preheparin LPL mass is not active, and the role of this serum LPL mass is not known. We sought to clarify the role of LPL. The administration of troglitazone, an insulin-sensitizer, increased the serum LPL mass, as did an insulin injection. Metabolic syndrome severity is negatively related to serum LPL mass. The homeostasis model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR) is also correlated with serum LPL mass. These data suggest that the LPL mass reflects the LPL production in the entire body and might be a good marker of insulin sensitivity. Working from this assumption, we have assessed the effects of various lipid- and glucose-targeting drugs on LPL mass and have found several interesting results. 1) Metformin, which is mainly thought to reduce glucose synthesis in the liver, increased the serum LPL mass in type 2 diabetes mellitus patients. Thus, we observed a previously unidentified effect of metformin. In an in vitro study, metformin enhanced the LPL produced by skeletal muscle cells through the activation of adenosine monophosphate-activated protein kinase (AMPK); however, this effect was not observed in adipocytes. 2) 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A (HMG-CoA) reductase inhibitors (statins) are widely used to reduce serum low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) levels. Some statins reduce serum TG levels and increase serum high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) levels. Pravastatin and atorvastatin increase preheparin LPL mass in patients with type 2 diabetes. Simvastatin and pitavastatin increase LPL activity in adipocytes. In skeletal muscle cells, atorvastatin and pitavastatin enhance LPL production through the activation of AMPK. This review will define serum LPL mass and describe the effects of several lipid- and glucose-targeting drugs on serum LPL mass, in addition to discussing new mechanisms regulating triglyceride metabolism in adipocytes and skeletal muscle cells. © 2013 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
