TI  - Is the adipose tissue a relevant target for obesity treatment?
PY  - 2013
T2  - Physiology and Physiopathology of Adipose Tissue
VL  - 9782817803432
SP  - 397
EP  - 431
DO  - 10.1007/978-2-8178-0343-2_26
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84930017717&doi=10.1007%2f978-2-8178-0343-2_26&partnerID=40&md5=9ccb1efc8e8923217fe3d7b6e74fce96
AB  - Adipocyte and adipose tissue dysfunction (AT) may link some obesity phenotypes to increased risk of metabolic syndrome. AT dysfunction is characterized by increased lipid storage, hypoxia, impaired insulin sensitivity in adipocytes, secretion of proinflammatory cytokines, fibrosis, and changes in the cellular composition. When expansion of the AT reaches its maximum limit, fat accumulates in non-adipose tissues such as muscle and liver. This alteration in fatty acid trafficking, that is a consequence of AT dysfunction, is a key feature distinguishing metabolically healthy (MHO) from metabolically abnormal obese (MAO) patients. It results in lipotoxic insults including insulin resistance, inflammation, and apoptosis. We described an acquired partial lipodystrophy syndrome, which is closely linkedwith the severity of metabolic complications. This phenotype has been described also in MAO children and in the elderly population. Anti-obesity treatment strategies even with moderate reduction of fat mass may reverse unhealthy into healthy obesity: according to the AT expandability hypothesis, the weight loss program has to be tailored to an individual's threshold of metabolic complications. Remodeling of dysfunctional AT into a healthier tissue seems to be another promising new approach to treat MAO patients. Some drugs could have a direct effect on AT inflammation and cytokine secretion. © 2013 Springer-Verlag France. All rights reserved.
