TI  - Caloric restriction and/or increasing exercise in the management of obesity
PY  - 2013
T2  - Exercise Therapy in Adult Individuals with Obesity
SP  - 43
EP  - 72
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84895387880&partnerID=40&md5=1b92996e2dd1a43e7eda0f1ea69e3caa
AB  - It is now accepted that obesity is one of the main threats to human health in the twenty-first century. Obesity increases the risk of morbidity and mortality, and reduces the quality of life of individuals, independent of age, gender, or ethnicity. The total number of people with obesity worldwide was estimated at 396 million in 2005 and is projected to increase to 1.2 billion in 2030 if secular trends continue [1]. This global problem is associated with changes in lifestyle, including quantity and quality of diet, and decreased levels of physical activity. This chapter summarizes current knowledge regarding the health benefits of caloric restriction and increasing exercise in the treatment of overweight and obese adults. In particular, body composition, cardiovascular disease risk factors, disease incidence, psychosocial factors, and quality of life are considered. © 2013 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
