TI  - Osteocalcin: A link between bone, energy metabolism and disease
PY  - 2012
T2  - Osteocalcin: Production, Regulation and Disease
SP  - 151
EP  - 160
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84895205235&partnerID=40&md5=beea07f404126b0022f874223485a7b6
AB  - Osteocalcin, also known as gamma-carboxyglutamic acid-containing protein or bone gla protein is a small, non-collagenous and highly conserved and secreted protein that is associated with the mineralized matrix of bone. Originally identified in chicken bone, this protein is encoded by the BGLAP (bone gamma-carboxyglutamate protein) gene in human and by the OG1 (osteocalcin gene 1) and OG2 (osteocalcin gene 2) genes in mouse. The osteocalcin protein is expressed at different levels in osteoblasts, chondrocytes, odontoblasts, cementoblasts and cementocytes, however the osteocalcin transcript is reported in ovary, kidney, pancreas, spleen, thymus, prostate, and testis, except bone marrow. Osteocalcin is a well-known determinant of bone formation. Its bone expression, considered to be a specific marker for late osteoblast differentiation, is influenced by various growth factors, hormones, and vitamins including bFGF, IGF-1, TGF-beta, TNF-alpha, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3, endothelin-1, calcitonin, parathyroid hormone, thyroxine (T4) thyroid hormone, tri-iodothyronine (T3) thyroid hormone, glucocorticoid and progesterone. Osteocalcin is classically associated with the regulation of energy homeostasis, such as in the pancreas and adipose tissue. In gonads, osteocalcin is considered as an endocrine regulator of reproduction, by regulating the testosterone synthesis and promoting the germ cell survival. Therefore, the nature of the osteocalcin implication in numerous pathways regulating metabolism and its intervention in crosstalk between energy metabolism, nutrition and bone metabolism, open several potential applications related to this protein in the treatment of metabolic diseases. © 2012 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
