AB  - Hyperferritinaemia with normal or mildly elevated transferrin saturation is observed in approximately one-third of patients with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), and has been named the 'dysmetabolic iron overload syndrome' (DIOS). Current evidence suggests that elevated body iron stores exert a detrimental effect on the clinical course of obesity-related conditions, and that iron removal improves insulin sensitivity and delays the onset of type 2 diabetes. Marginal copper deficiency together with increased fructose consumption may interact with and augment iron perturbations. This review summarises the current knowledge about the dysregulation of iron homeostasis in NAFLD and highlights the rationale for its therapeutic implications. © TOUCH BRIEFINGS 2012.
