TY  - CHAP
AU  - del Prato, S.
TI  - Pathophysiology of Insulin Action in Humans
PY  - 2005
T2  - The Metabolic Syndrome at the Beginning of the XXI Century: A Genetic and Molecular Approach
SP  - 179
EP  - 197
DO  - 10.1016/B978-84-8174-892-5.50011-5
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33747314517&doi=10.1016%2fB978-84-8174-892-5.50011-5&partnerID=40&md5=cf659f7d394e74ca6dac93dfef2f6e45
AB  - Insulin is the key hormone in the regulation of glucose homeostasis. This action is the result of a fine tuned feedback loop between insulin secretion and plasma glucose concentration that incorporates insulin sensitivity as well, i.e. the ability of insulin to promote glucose disposal in the insulin-sensitive tissues. Insulin resistance has been recognized as a main pathogenetic factor in the development of type 2 diabetes and a feature of obesity. In the past years much information has been cumulated with respect to the physiology as well as the cell biology and molecular genetics of insulin action. More recently it has been suggested that insulin resistance may play a pivotal role in pathogenesis of the Metabolic Syndrome. Insulin resistance has been associated with dyslipidemia, hypertension, endothelial dysfunction, inflammation, coagulative state and so on. The comprehension of the possible role of impaired insulin action in pathogenesis of the Metabolic Syndrome requires the full understanding of the multiplicity of action of insulin. Although insulin sensitivity commonly refers to the ability of insulin to promote glucose utilization, the hormone exerts a wide pleiotropic action. Minute changes in plasma insulin concentration exert a powerful effect on lipolysis and lipid metabolism. Moreover, insulin affects ion metabolism, endothelial function, platelet aggregation and coagulation, thermogenesis, heart function. Though the central nervous system is considered to be a non-insulin dependent tissue, insulin exerts important actions within the brain. Finally, preliminary data suggest that insulin may directly influence function and survival of the beta-cell. It is this finely entangled network of action of the hormone that, very likely, underlies the many alterations associated with impairment of insulin-mediated glucose utilization and that concur in determining the abnormalities clustering in the Metabolic Syndrome. © 2005 Elsevier España, S.A.
