EP  - 145
DO  - 10.1097/00003081-200403000-00017
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-1842536517&doi=10.1097%2f00003081-200403000-00017&partnerID=40&md5=7977c4e7a6067481c733d17a4d1fd14d
AB  - Multifetal pregnancy reduction will remain an option for care in high order multiple gestations. As with most medical problems, primary prevention would be more satisfactory than facing this secondary prevention conundrum. It is critical that physicians who treat infertile couples continue to work toward reducing the risk of multiple gestations. The outcomes of triplet and quadruplet gestations reduced to twins are generally good. Many factors affect the outcome, including the psychological outcome for the woman and her partner. A careful explanation of the risks of doing the procedure, as well as the risks of not doing the procedure is critical to the woman's ability to make an informed decision. If she elects to undergo MFPR then her pregnancy will continue to need close evaluation as she is still at increased risk of pregnancy complications. Supportive guidance throughout her pregnancy and post-partum period is needed. Multifetal pregnancy reduction has allowed many couples to achieve their goal of parenthood of healthy children. The balance of the joys of pregnancy and the pain of this decision is difficult, but usually successful.
