SP  - 383
EP  - 393
DO  - 10.1016/B978-84-8174-892-5.50024-3
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84941264557&doi=10.1016%2fB978-84-8174-892-5.50024-3&partnerID=40&md5=1d76646d0ce2d72b71b71a535b58eaee
AB  - There is increasing evidence that the clinical disorder termed nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is closely associated with the Meta bolic Syndrome. It is currently under investigation whether NAFLD represents a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease. Its main characteristics comprise elevated serum liver enzymes, an altered serum lipid profile and accumulation of fat in the liver. Several pathophysiologic mechanisms may underlie this syndrome. Among them insulin resistance of peripheral tissues, hepatic insulin resistance and an imbalance in serum adipokines are the predominant ones. Lifestyle intervention programs and pharmacotherapy that mainly target individuals with NAFLD were also found to affect classical characteristics of the Metabolic Syndrome. Therefore, early screening for this disorder and intervention may be effective in preventing manifestation of the Metabolic Syndrome. © 2005 Elsevier España, S.A.
